 34MG c  First in-beam |g-ray spectroscopy of {+34}Mg.                          
 34MG c  2001Yo03, 2002Yo04: a 95-MeV/nucleon, 60-pnA primary beam of {+40}Ar   
 34MG2c  bombarded a 462.5-mg/cm2 {+9}Be primary target. A secondary beam of    
 34MG3c  {+36}Si was separated by the the RIKEN projectile-fragment separator   
 34MG4c  RIPS and transported to a secondary target of 385.5-mg/cm2 {+9}Be at a 
 34MG5c  center-of-target energy of 38 MeV/nucleon. Reaction products from the  
 34MG6c  36Si+9Be fragmentation were detected and identified at forward angles  
 34MG7c  with a parallel plate avalanche counter (PPAC) and four sets of counter
 34MG8c  telescopes, each consisting of three silicon detectors and one Si(Li)  
 34MG9c  detector. |g rays emitted from excited fragments were detected using 66
 34MGAc  NaI(Tl) detectors. Measured E|g with Doppler correction, I|g,          
 34MGBc  ({+34}Mg)|g-coin, and |g|g-coin. Deduced levels. Also see review       
 34MGCc  articles 2002Sa11 and 2002Mo35.                                        
 34MG cG E$From 2001Yo03.                                                       
 34MG cL E$From a least-squares fit to |g-ray energies. The observed energies of
 34MG2cL the first 4+ level and the first 2+ level yield an R{-4/2}=3.2, which  
 34MG3cL is very close to 10/3 expected for a rigid roter and 34Mg might be a   
 34MG4cL nucleus with well-developed quadrupole deformation (2001Yo03).         
 34MG cL J$Assigned based on fragmentation reaction systematics of magnesium    
 34MG2cL isotopes (2001Yo03).                                                   
